/* Legacy theme's option (settings) window content -- frame/header/positioning come from
   window_shell.rcss; this file only lays out the rows spliced into its #content slot.

   Lives here (themes/legacy/), NOT at the RmlUi/ root -- option_window.rml's own
   <link href="option_window.rcss"/> resolves against LoadThemedDocument()'s sourceUrl
   (RmlTheme.cpp), which is always "Data/Interface/RmlUi/themes/<theme>/<name>.rml" regardless of
   which path the RML's own bytes were actually read from. A root-level copy is never reachable by
   that link for the legacy theme. login.rml's own "shared markup, no themed override needed"
   shortcut only applies to falling back to the .rml file itself; it does NOT extend to where a
   <link>'s href then resolves from.

   #panel is a genuine real-dp size, NOT native COptionWindow's own reference-resolution rect
   (190x419) -- this template assumes no resolution-based rescaling, so copying native's
   reference-pixel constants here would produce a wildly oversized panel once combined with
   window_shell's own chrome.

   Width is 230dp, NOT an arbitrary/bigger guess -- window_shell.rcss's msgbox sprite chrome
   (top/middle/bottom caps + back-fill) is fixed sprite art authored at exactly this width
   (generic_menu_dialog.rcss's own #panel matches it too, "native's own MSGBOX_WIDTH"); the caps'
   left/right border art gets visibly stretched/distorted at any other width. .window-shell-sprite-
   middle is a single stretched flat fill by design (its own comment), so height is free to vary
   with content -- only WIDTH must stay locked to the sprite's real size. Positioning is pure CSS
   (.center-both) now that dragging is off, so this file alone owns the panel's size.

   Height (276dp) is sized for the tallest tab (Graphics, 6 checkbox rows: 6*24dp=144dp, at the
   18dp row height below + 6dp margin-bottom) plus the tab bar (~28dp) and the always-visible close
   button block (~38dp), inside window_shell.rcss's own titled-panel insets (35dp top + 28dp bottom
   = 63dp): 144+28+38+63=273dp minimum, +3dp buffer. Content splits across tabs, so only one tab's
   rows need to fit at a time. */
/* font-family/size/color set here, not per-row -- every sibling legacy document does the same
   (login.rcss, generic_menu_dialog.rcss's own #panel rule) because base.rcss's own .checkbox-label
   sets no font of its own, unlike the modern theme's copy (which does) -- without this, checkbox
   labels rendered at RmlUi's uncontrolled default size/font instead of something sized to fit the
   row heights below, overlapping/misaligning against the checkbox glyph and neighboring rows.
   10dp -- this theme's other windows (my_quest_info/generic_menu_dialog/login all use 11-12px/dp
   for their own primary body text; base.rcss's 9dp is that shared baseline's SMALL-control size --
   checkbox/button labels -- not what a dialog's own readable content should inherit). Not raised
   all the way to 11-12dp: #panel stays locked at 230dp (sprite-constrained, see its own comment
   below), and this
   window's label column already runs close to that width's limit with its longer setting names
   (see .option-row-label's own comment) -- 10dp is the largest step that didn't need also
   shortening more label text than the two already trimmed in Game.en.resx. */
#panel {
	width: 230dp;
	height: 276dp;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-size: 1rem;
	color: token(text-input);
}

/* Widens the control column from window_shell.rcss's own shared 32dp/side margin (166dp well,
   leaving only 88dp for a slider/select control after the 78dp label column -- too narrow for
   dropdown text like a language name or "1920 x 1080"). An ID selector, not a re-declared
   .window-shell-content, so it wins regardless of <link> merge order between this file and
   window_shell.rcss's own (both are plain class-selector rules at equal specificity otherwise).
   #panel stays locked at 230dp (see its own comment) -- this reclaims width from the shared
   margin instead of growing the sprite-constrained frame. 10dp/side so the control column below
   can start nearer the row's own center, not just "wide enough" -- new well width: 230-20=210dp. */
#content {
	left: 10dp;
	right: 10dp;
}

/* One row of tab buttons -- reuses my_quest_info.rcss's own tab sprite family
   (Quest_tab01/02.tga) rather than a hand-rolled boxed-button look, per that window already being
   the established "tabbed legacy window" reference. Not a pixel-for-pixel port though:
   my_quest_info.rcss's own .tab-row/.tab-btn are fixed reference-px position:absolute with
   3 unequal-width tabs (48/48/72px) hand-placed to match its own fixed 190px frame -- this window
   has 6 equal-width tabs across a differently-sized flex/dp row, needing each sprite resized to a
   box different from its own native pixel size. `decorator: image(...)` does NOT stretch a sprite
   to a mismatched box in this RmlUi build (most of the box stays unpainted instead of the art
   scaling); `ninepatch(...)` does, correctly, in both directions, same fix already used for
   .gmd-btn.cols-2/.server-row's own resized reuse of a fixed-size sprite. Inner rects below are a
   conservative uniform inset (not pixel-verified against the source art, unlike legacy-btn-idle's
   own inner rect) -- revisit if the stretched edges show visible seams/artifacts. Redeclared
   spritesheets (RmlUi spritesheets are per-document, can't share my_quest_info.rcss's own). */
@spritesheet option-tab-back-sheet   { src: "../../../Quest_tab01.tga"; option-tab-back:   0px 0px 166px 22px; option-tab-back-inner:   6px 4px 154px 14px; }
@spritesheet option-tab-active-sheet { src: "../../../Quest_tab02.tga"; option-tab-active: 0px 0px 48px 22px;  option-tab-active-inner: 4px 4px 40px 14px; }
.option-tab-row {
	display: flex;
	width: 100%;
	height: 22dp;
	margin-bottom: 8dp;
	decorator: ninepatch(option-tab-back, option-tab-back-inner);
}
/* Sized by their labels (not six equal cells): "Gameplay" and "Graphics" need more room than
   "UI", and equal cells cut them off. */
.option-tab-btn {
	flex: 1 1 auto;
	padding: 0 2dp;
	height: 22dp;
	line-height: 22dp;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 1rem;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	color: token(text-muted);
}
.option-tab-btn:hover {
	color: token(text-input);
}
.option-tab-btn.active-tab {
	color: token(text-primary);
	decorator: ninepatch(option-tab-active, option-tab-active-inner);
}

/* Each tab's row group -- a flex column of its own, same as #content itself, so its own rows
   still stack top-to-bottom once spliced in. Needs the .hidden override below: this file loads
   after base.rcss, so its own display:flex would otherwise tie with (and silently beat) base.rcss's
   global .hidden{display:none} at equal specificity, leaving a "hidden" tab visibly rendered --
   the exact bug window_shell.rcss's own .window-shell-header-rail.hidden rule already fixed once
   for its header rail; same fix, same reason. */
.option-tab-panel {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	width: 100%;
}
.option-tab-panel.hidden {
	display: none;
}

/* Each settings row is a small flex line inside window_shell's own flex-column #content -- native
   used fixed per-row px offsets; this reproduces the same top-to-bottom order via normal flow
   instead, keeping row order traceable from the markup rather than from literal numbers. */
.option-row {
	position: relative;
	width: 100%;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	flex-shrink: 0;
	margin-bottom: 6dp;
}
.option-checkbox-row {
	height: 18dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
/* Not base.rcss's own .checkbox-label -- that's position:absolute, glyph-first (built for
   login.rml's standalone checkboxes), which doesn't line up with a column of sibling rows. This
   row uses .option-row-label (below) instead, label-first in the markup, same as every other row
   type -- see option_window.rml's own comment. */
.checkbox-box {
	flex-shrink: 0;
}
/* Tick is baked into the checked-state sprite art (base.rcss's .checkbox-box.checked), same as
   login.rcss's own legacy checkbox -- .checkbox-tick draws nothing here. */
.checkbox-tick {
	display: none;
}

/* 116dp -- widened from 100dp alongside the 9dp->10dp font bump above, both to keep the control
   column starting near the row's own horizontal center (well width 210dp, see #content's own
   comment) and to give the longer Graphics-tab labels room at the bigger size. The two longest
   labels in this window ("Disable Skill Effect Models"/"Disable Ambient Wildlife") were shortened
   in Game.en.resx to "Disable Skill Effects"/"Disable Wildlife" (value only -- the resx `name`,
   and therefore the generated I18N::Game::* identifier, is unchanged) since even 116dp couldn't
   fit their original full length at 10dp without either shrinking the control column to
   uselessness or wrapping to two lines (no per-row auto-height support here, see .option-row's own
   comment). New control width: 210-116=94dp. */
.option-row-label {
	width: 116dp;
	flex-shrink: 0;
	white-space: nowrap;
	color: token(text-input);
}

.option-slider-row,
.option-select-row {
	height: 20dp;
}

/* First real use of RmlUi's native <input type="range"> in this codebase -- drives the same
   WidgetSlider class as scrollbars, styled via the same slidertrack/sliderbar sub-elements
   generic_confirm_dialog.rcss already proves render/receive-input correctly in this build. Arrow
   sub-elements collapsed to 0 like that file's own scrollbar rule -- drag/click-only, matching
   native's own slider affordance (no stepper buttons).

   pointer-events: auto is required directly on this element, not just an ancestor -- same reason
   generic_confirm_dialog.rcss's own .gcd-text-col states it explicitly right next to its
   scrollbar rules: base.rcss's `body { pointer-events: none; }` inherits down to every descendant
   that doesn't opt back in, and WidgetSlider's generated slidertrack/sliderbar are real DOM
   children of THIS element (the <input> itself), inheriting whatever ITS OWN computed value is.
   .window-shell-content resetting to auto further up the tree covers ordinary rows fine, but
   leaving this un-stated here relies on that inheritance chain never getting interrupted anywhere
   in between -- without it explicitly set here, the slider never moves on drag. */
.option-slider {
	flex-grow: 1;
	height: 12dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.option-slider slidertrack {
	height: 4dp;
	background-color: token(surface-recessed);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-control);
}
.option-slider sliderbar {
	width: 10dp;
	background-color: #8f6922;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #d3aa4e, #8f6922);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #aa8132;
}
.option-slider sliderbar:hover,
.option-slider sliderbar:active {
	background-color: #a67c2c;
}
.option-slider sliderarrowdec,
.option-slider sliderarrowinc {
	width: 0;
	height: 0;
}

/* Custom dropdown -- NOT RmlUi's native <select>/<option> (WidgetDropDown). That was tried first
   and never reliably worked in this engine: the widget's own generated
   selectvalue/selectbox/selectarrow sub-elements need CSS this
   theme never fully gave them (no `position` set programmatically the way WidgetSlider forces
   `drag` on its own sub-elements), and even once styled, option clicks still didn't reliably reach
   this window's own change callback. This reuses the exact data-for/data-class/data-event-click
   mechanism .option-tab-btn above already proves reliable, plus a single shared
   model.openDropdown field (OptionWindow.h) so opening one dropdown closes any other automatically
   -- no separate exclusivity logic needed. position:relative so .option-dropdown-list's own
   position:absolute (below) resolves against ITS box. */
.option-dropdown {
	position: relative;
	flex-grow: 1;
	height: 18dp;
}
/* border-box: width 100% plus the padding and border ran past the frame's right edge. */
.option-dropdown-value {
	display: block;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	padding: 0 4dp;
	line-height: 18dp;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	background-color: token(surface-control);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-control);
	color: token(text-input);
}
/* Floating popup -- top:100% (not a hardcoded dp offset) so it always sits flush under the value
   box regardless of that row's own height. max-height+overflow-y:auto caps a long list
   (Resolution has ~25 entries) with a real scrollbar instead of growing past the panel. Needs the
   .hidden override below: this file loads after base.rcss, so its own display:block would
   otherwise tie with (and silently beat) base.rcss's global .hidden{display:none} at equal
   specificity -- same cascade-tie bug .option-tab-panel's own comment already covers.

   z-index:1 -- without it, this absolutely-positioned popup paints/hit-tests at its own plain DOM
   position among .option-row siblings, i.e. BEHIND whichever row comes later in markup order (an
   .option-row without its own z-index just participates in normal top-to-bottom paint order, same
   as any other sibling): a dropdown opened above a lower row would render behind it, and clicking
   an option that visually overlaps a checkbox row would trigger that checkbox instead.
   RmlUi's own native <select> (WidgetDropDown.cpp) sets this same 1 on its generated
   selectbox for the identical reason -- this is the custom-dropdown equivalent of that. */
.option-dropdown-list {
	display: block;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 100%;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 100%;
	max-height: 90dp;
	overflow-y: auto;
	z-index: 1;
	background-color: token(surface-control);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-control);
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.option-dropdown-list.hidden {
	display: none;
}
/* Explicit scrollbar sizing, same reasoning generic_confirm_dialog.rcss's own .gcd-text-col
   comment already gives for its own overflow:auto well -- an unstyled scrollbarvertical isn't
   just visually wrong, its own (also unstyled) hit-test area can cover most/all of the option
   row's width instead of a slim strip at the edge, silently swallowing clicks meant for the
   option text beneath it as a scrollbar drag instead. Arrow buttons collapsed to 0, same
   drag/click-only affordance as every other scrollbar in this codebase. */
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical {
	width: 6dp;
	margin: 0;
}
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical slidertrack {
	background-color: token(surface-recessed);
}
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical sliderbar {
	background-color: token(control-thumb);
}
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical sliderbar:hover,
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical sliderbar:active {
	background-color: token(control-thumb-hover);
}
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical sliderarrowdec,
.option-dropdown-list scrollbarvertical sliderarrowinc {
	width: 0;
	height: 0;
}
.option-dropdown-option {
	display: block;
	width: 100%;
	padding: 2dp 4dp;
	white-space: nowrap;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	color: token(text-input);
}
.option-dropdown-option:hover {
	color: token(text-primary);
	background-color: token(border-control);
}
.option-dropdown-option.selected {
	color: token(text-primary);
	background-color: #8f6922;
}

/* Hover tooltip for a row whose label can't explain itself (the UI Scale row). Declarative
   `:hover`, no C++ hover callback, the same mechanism main_frame.rcss's gauge tooltips use. This
   theme declares the whole look here because its base.rcss has no shared .tooltip shell (the
   modern theme's does, so its copy of this file only positions the element); the values mirror
   main_frame.rcss's own .tooltip rule with this window's border colour.

   pointer-events must be stated on the ROW: base.rcss's `body { pointer-events: none; }` reaches
   every element that doesn't opt back in, and an .option-select-row (unlike .option-checkbox-row)
   doesn't otherwise need it -- without it, hovering the label area produces no :hover and the tip
   appears only over the dropdown box. The tip itself stays pointer-events:none so it can't eat the
   hover showing it.

   The tip sits BELOW the row (top:100%) and that row's dropdown list ABOVE it (bottom:100%), the
   reverse of every other row here: this is the last row of its tab, so a 9-entry list opening
   downwards covers the panel's bottom frame art and its close button. Flipping the list up puts it
   over the rows above instead, and moving the tip down keeps the two apart. white-space stays
   default and no height is set -- the sentence is longer than the 210dp content well and wraps.
   z-index 2 keeps the tip over an open list (z-index 1). */
.option-row-tipped {
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.option-row-tip {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 100%;
	width: 190dp;
	z-index: 2;
	padding: 2dp 4dp;
	background-color: token(surface-tooltip);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-control);
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	color: token(text-primary);
	font-size: 1rem;
	pointer-events: none;
}
.option-row-tipped:hover .option-row-tip {
	display: block;
}
.option-row-tipped .option-dropdown-list {
	top: auto;
	bottom: 100%;
}

/* Not authored in option_window.rml at all -- COptionWindow::BuildRmlUi() builds this element
   directly in C++ inside window_shell.rml's own #window_shell_footer (a `{{close_label}}` version
   of this div, moved into the footer after load, never rendered its label -- see
   OptionWindow.h's own comment on m_pCloseButtonEl). .btn already defaults to position:absolute
   (base.rcss) -- kept, not overridden to static, since the close button is meant to sit at a
   FIXED spot regardless of which tab is active, not flow after whatever that tab's own last row
   happens to be (which would otherwise shift its position as the active tab's row count changes).
   bottom:0 here resolves against the footer's own box (window_shell.rcss's own
   .window-shell-footer, itself position:absolute against #panel). left:50%+transform centers it
   horizontally the same way .center-both does. */
.option-close-btn {
	left: 50%;
	bottom: 0;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
}

/* The original's options window has no title banner, only the plain top cap: the shell's title
   (bound to "Option" for the modern theme) is not shown here. It used to be invisible by accident
   (its font was an unsubstituted token in window_shell.rcss). */
.window-shell-header-rail,
.window-shell-header-title { display: none; }
